1.48.m:
It is the realm of great-souled rākṣasas who assume forms at will,
the dwelling of those proud of their strength, known as enemies of the gods;
uncrowded by human beings, that great city is indeed inaccessible.

1.49.m:
The Sūta said:
I shall describe Plakṣadvīpa concisely, as it really is;
hear this in accordance with the truth as I speak, O best of the twice-born.

Now I shall tell of the seven great mountains of Plakṣadvīpa:
Gomedaka is the first mountain here, resembling a cloud;
indeed, that realm is known as Gomedaka after its name.

The fourth mountain there is indeed Dundubhi by name;
on it in ancient times Dundubhi, whose death was sound, was beaten by the gods.
[There is] Rajjudāra, made of rope, a silk-cotton tree that destroys asuras.

That of Candra is named Śikhara; that of Nārada is Sukhodaya;
Ānanda is the realm of Dundubhi; that of Somaka is remembered as Śiva;
Kṣemaka is that of Ṛṣabha, and likewise Dhruva is that of Vaibhrāja.

There are meritorious realms endowed with the four social classes.
In them are just seven rivers flowing to the ocean, one in each realm;
know them all by name, recorded as the sevenfold Gaṅgā.

1.50.m:
Fire and water have white shadows, and the earth has a dark shadow.
Because of the sun's distance when rising, it is devoid of rays;
from the absence of rays it has a red appearance, and from being red it lacks heat.

In the northern half of the earth and likewise in the southern,
when the sun rises, night enters the waters;
therefore the waters become copper-colored by day from the entrance of the night.

Nāgavīthī is the northern lane, and Ajavīthī is the southern.
Mūla and the two Āṣāḍhās are the three risings of Ajavīthī;
Abhijit, the former [Pūrvāṣāḍhā], and Svāti are the three risings of Nāgavīthī.

Three crores of rākṣasas, renowned by the name Mandēhas,
assail the thousand-rayed sun day after day as it rises;
tormenting him, the evil-souled ones seek to devour the sun.

Then a very fierce battle took place between them and the sun;
then Brahmā, the gods, and the best of brāhmaṇas,
worshipping [at the time called] sandhyā, throw sanctified water.

1.51.m:
The Sūta said:
In the Svāyambhuva creation, subsequent matters were explained;
I shall now declare the sequence of all those yet to come.
